{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 22, :comments ["<b>And to cleave to Him</b>. Rabbi Abraham (Ibn Ezra on Deuteronomy 11:22) said at the end, and it is a great secret. But the secret is not from the standpoint of this verse. Perhaps he means: to love HaShem, to walk in all His ways — until you become worthy to cleave to Him at the end. And it is stated in Joshua (Joshua 23:7–8): and by the name of their elohim you shall not mention, and you shall not make them swear, and you shall not serve them, and you shall not bow down to them, but in HaShem your Elohim you shall cleave, as you have done until this day. If so, it is a warning among the warnings against idolatry, that one's thought should not depart from HaShem to other elohim, so that one should not suppose that there is any essential reality in idolatry, but rather all is naught and nothingness. Now this is as He said further (Deuteronomy 13:5): and Him you shall serve and in Him you shall cleave — and the intent is to warn that one should not serve HaShem and something besides Him, but HaShem alone should one serve, in one's heart and in one's deeds. And it is possible that the cleaving is stated comprehensively, to mean: you should remember HaShem and His love constantly; do not let your thought depart from Him when you walk on the way, when you lie down, and when you rise — until your words with people are on your lips and your tongue while your heart is not with them but rather before HaShem. And it is possible, regarding men of this exalted level, that their soul is bound, even in their lifetime, in the bond of life, for they themselves are a dwelling-place for the Shekhinah, as the author of the Sefer ha-Kuzari hinted. And I have already mentioned something of this in the portion of the forbidden relations (Leviticus 18:4 [n]). And what Joshua said — <b>as you have done until this day</b> — is because while they were in the wilderness, with the cloud of HaShem upon them, and the manna descending from heaven, and the quail coming up, and the well before them constantly, and all their needs in the hands of heaven through miraculous matters — behold, their thought and their deeds were with HaShem constantly. Therefore Joshua warns them that now too, in the land, when those wondrous deeds have departed from them, their thought should nonetheless remain constantly upon them, to cleave to the honored and awesome Name, and their intent should not depart from HaShem."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {0 "Ramban cross-references his own earlier comment at Leviticus 18:4 (in parashat ha-Arayot); the reference is to his comment there on the phrase 'you shall do My ordinances,' where he discusses cleaving to God."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T02:48:13.119684047Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}